WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:07.480
The normal understanding is that you trust
a theory — the only reason to trust a theory

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:13.269
is that the theory has been confirmed empirically,
which means that the theory makes predictions

00:00:13.269 --> 00:00:22.659
and those predictions then turn out to be
in agreement with empirical data. This means

00:00:22.659 --> 00:00:31.289
that any theory that hasn’t found empirical
confirmation must be considered a pure speculation.

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:35.500
And this seems to be not quite right. There
are theories that haven’t found empirical

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:43.550
confirmation but which are trusted quite a
lot by physicists — for example, string

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:50.390
theory. String theory hasn’t found any empirical
confirmation but many string theorists think

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:57.050
they have very good reasons for trusting the
theory. And I want to understand this based

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:03.780
on the concept of what I call non-empirical
confirmation. The idea is that there are observations

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:12.609
about the context of scientific inquiry, about
the development of a theory and so on, and

00:01:12.609 --> 00:01:16.329
those observations can generate trust in the
theory.

